# One accepted finding. Everything else must fail the scan.
#
# WHY A COMMIT FINGERPRINT AND NOT A PATH ALLOWLIST. `security.yml` runs gitleaks in git
# mode with fetch-depth 0, which reports a secret ONCE, at the commit that introduced it,
# not in every descendant. A commit is immutable, so this fingerprint can never drift --
# unlike a fingerprint for a finding at HEAD, which stops matching when the line moves.
# A path allowlist would blind the scanner to every FUTURE secret in that file, forever;
# this accepts exactly one known finding and nothing else. (Mechanism confirmed by acmp,
# who owns the shared workflow; the workflow's own error text pointed the other way and is
# being corrected.)
#
# WHAT IT IS: a base64 password for the DatabaseUser that
# `tests/ga/chains/database.yaml` creates and deletes inside a single GA chain run. Not an
# account credential, and not a credential for anything that outlives the run.
#
# FIXED FORWARD IN 3e68515, not suppressed. The chain now takes the value from the
# operator's shell as `${DB_USER_PASSWORD_B64}`, declared empty so the run refuses rather
# than quietly using a credential out of git. `vpn.yaml`'s `${PSK_SECRET}` was already
# doing this correctly; database.yaml was the one outlier.
#
# A SECOND COPY WAS FOUND BY LOOKING FOR IT. The same value, with its plaintext beside it,
# was also in `docs/live-cluster-test.md:966` -- which the scanner did NOT report, because
# it reports only the introducing commit and that doc copy came later. Fixing the file the
# scan named would have left the value in the repo. Anyone accepting a finding here should
# grep the working tree for the value first; that check is what turned one fix into two.
#
# NOT DONE, deliberately: no history rewrite. It would invalidate every SHA in a repo with
# 42 merged PRs and break pins other work quotes, to remove a throwaway test password.
